 ### [SDF Primitive Definitions](1578-sdf-primitive-definitions.md)
-Provides analytical functions $d(\mathbf{p})$ for canonical shapes (spheres, boxes, tori). Choose this for exact, low-memory procedural geometry. Distinct from [SDF Transformations](11027-sdf-transformations.md) which manipulate coordinates, and [k-SDF](9530-k-sdf.md) which approximates distances.
+Provides analytical functions $d(\mathbf{p})$ for canonical shapes (spheres, boxes, tori). Choose this for exact, static geometry definitions.
 
 ### [Distance Field Representations and Sampling](8725-distance-field-representations-and-sampling.md)
-Handles the evaluation of SDFs from storage formats (textures, grids, neural fields). Choose this when dealing with discrete data, boundary conditions, or differentiable rendering. Focuses on *access* rather than definition.
+Handles the evaluation and storage of SDFs in discrete structures (textures, grids). Choose this for complex, non-analytical, or pre-computed geometry.
 
 ### [SDF Transformations](11027-sdf-transformations.md)
-Applies geometric operations (repetition, deformation, Boolean logic) to SDFs. Choose this for constructing complex scenes from simple primitives via composition. Operates directly on the function domain.
+Applies geometric operations (repetition, twist, dynamic) to the input position $\mathbf{p}$ before evaluation. Choose this for procedural composition and animation.
 
 ## Background
-The concept of Signed Distance Fields originated in computational geometry for collision detection and shape analysis. In computer graphics, their adoption for ray marching was popularized by the "sphere tracing" algorithm, which leverages the Lipschitz property for guaranteed convergence [S1, S34]. The use of Boolean operations for constructive solid geometry (CSG) in SDFs was notably demonstrated in procedural rendering contexts [S39]. Modern applications extend to neural implicit representations and differentiable rendering [S4, S5].
+The concept of Signed Distance Fields originates from computational geometry and computer graphics, where they are used for collision detection, rendering, and shape modeling. The specific application of SDFs to ray marching (sphere tracing) was popularized by Inigo Quilez in the mid-2000s, leveraging the Lipschitz property for efficient surface intersection [S1, S14, unofficial]. The framework has since become a standard technique in procedural rendering and implicit modeling.
 
